{"kind":"stack","id":"skeleton-muscles","title":"Skeletons and muscles: support, protection and movement","theme":{"background":"#f6f8f2","foreground":"#1f3a2b","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Skeleton"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"The hard frame inside many animals that gives shape, support and protection."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Muscle"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Soft tissue that pulls on bones to make the body move."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Which part of your body protects your brain?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Your skull","why":"The skull is a hard bone case that wraps around and protects the brain.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Your ribs","why":"Ribs protect the heart and lungs, but a pupil may think they guard the brain.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Your kneecap","why":"A pupil may remember the kneecap as a protective bone and wrongly link it to the head.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"What do muscles do to bones to make them move?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Pull on them","why":"Muscles contract and pull on bones; they cannot push them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Push them away","why":"A pupil may think muscles push bones apart, but muscles can only pull.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"Melt them","why":"A pupil may think muscles soften bones, which is not true.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"You are out on a windy hill and a kite string snaps. Do you chase the kite or stay near the path?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"Chase the kite","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Stay on the path","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You run fast after the kite. Your leg muscles pull your leg bones, so you move quickly. Your skeleton keeps your legs straight."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"You stay on the path and walk carefully. Your skeleton supports your body, and your muscles help you keep your balance."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"What is the main job of a skeleton?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Support and protect the body","why":"A skeleton gives shape and support and protects organs like the heart and brain.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Make food for the body","why":"A pupil may mix up the skeleton with the digestive system, which processes food.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Carry blood around","why":"A pupil may mix up the skeleton with the circulatory system, which moves blood.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Which animal has a skeleton inside its body?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"A dog","why":"Dogs are vertebrates with an internal bony skeleton.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"A snail","why":"A pupil may think snails have a skeleton, but a snail has a shell and no internal bones.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"A worm","why":"A pupil may think worms have bones, but worms have no skeleton of bones.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which pair of body parts works together to make your arm bend?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Bones and muscles","why":"Muscles pull on bones, and together they move the arm.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Skin and hair","why":"A pupil may think skin and hair move the arm, but they do not work the bones.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Teeth and nails","why":"A pupil may mix up hard body parts with the parts that move the body.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"Well done! You finished the skeletons and muscles quiz."},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"You now know how skeletons support and protect us and how muscles help us move."}],"actions":[]}}
